// M6 Phase 6 regression fixture — `ynz run` signal-masking fix.
//
// Unbounded non-tail recursion with no `wait` point grows the native call stack until
// it exceeds the OS-mapped guard region, which the kernel reports to the process as a
// real, deterministic SIGSEGV (signal 11) — no compiler bug required. Compiled Yinz
// binaries link a plain C-style `main` (see `crates/ynz-driver/src/build.rs`'s linker
// invocation), so no Rust `std::rt` stack-overflow guard-page handler is installed for
// the process's main thread; the raw kernel SIGSEGV reaches `ynz run`'s child-process
// wait unmodified. Used by `tests/run_cli.rs` to prove `ynz run` reports "killed by
// signal 11 (SIGSEGV)" instead of masking the crash as exit code 1.
//
// v0.3-M7 Phase 3 repair: the original body was `return recurse(n + 1)` — a TAIL call,
// contradicting this fixture's own "non-tail" premise. It only overflowed because the
// pre-M7 default backend (-O0) performs no tail-call elimination; under the now-default
// `default<O2>` pipeline LLVM legally turns it into an infinite LOOP (no stack growth,
// no SIGSEGV, 100% CPU forever — it wedged the whole run_cli suite, which has no
// watchdog). The accumulate-AFTER-the-call form below is genuinely non-tail (the frame
// must survive the callee to add 1), so the stack grows and the kernel SIGSEGV fires at
// EVERY optimization tier (verified: exit 139 at both --no-optimize and the optimized
// default). The feature under test — signal reporting, not stack behavior — is unchanged.
function recurse(n: int) -> int {
  let deeper = recurse(n + 1)
  return deeper + 1
}

function entrypoint() -> nothing {
  recurse(0)
}
